[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m [response] To find the smallest possible value of the least common multiple (LCM) of \(a\) and \(b\), we start by noting that the units digit of \(a\) is 2 and the units digit of \(b\) is 4. This means that \(a\) and \(b\) can be written as \(a = 10x + 2\) and \(b = 10y + 4\) for some integers \(x\) and \(y\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m The greatest common divisor (GCD) of \(a\) and \(b\) is 6. This implies that both \(a\) and \(b\) must be multiples of 6. Therefore, we can express \(a\) and \(b\) as \(a = 6m\) and \(b = 6n\) for some integers \(m\) and \(n\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
