[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m First, we compute the derivative of the \( x \)-coordinate with respect to \( t \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \frac{d}{dt} \left( 3 \sin \frac{t}{4} \right) = 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \cdot \frac{1}{4} = \frac{3}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Next, we compute the derivative of the \( y \)-coordinate with respect to \( t \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \frac{d}{dt} \left( 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \right) = 3 \left( -\sin \frac{t}{4} \cdot \frac{1}{4} \right) = -\frac{3}{4} \sin \frac{t}{4}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m The speed \( v \) of the particle is the magnitude of the velocity vector:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m v = \sqrt{\left( \frac{3}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4} \right)^2 + \left( -\frac{3}{4} \sin \frac{t}{4} \right)^2} = \sqrt{\left( \frac{3}{4} \right)^2 \left( \cos^2 \frac{t}{4} + \sin^2 \frac{t}{4} \right)} = \sqrt{\left( \frac{3}{4} \right)^2 \cdot 1} = \frac{3}{4}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Therefore, the speed of the particle is \(\boxed{\frac{3}{4}}\) unit of distance per unit of time.
