[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m    Let the isosceles triangle have sides \(a\) and \(a\) (since it's isosceles), and the base \(b\). The altitude from the vertex angle to the base divides the base into two equal segments of \(b/2\). The altitude also forms a right angle with the base, so the altitude is the geometric mean of the two equal segments of the base, i.e., \(h = \sqrt{ab}\).
