[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m First, let's convert the first gear's rotation rate into degrees per second:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ 33 \frac{1}{3} = \frac{100}{3} \text{ degrees per minute} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m So, the first gear's rotation rate is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ \frac{100}{3} \times 60 = 2000 \text{ degrees per minute} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Next, let's convert the second gear's rotation rate into degrees per second:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ 45 \text{ degrees per minute} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m The relative speed of the two gears is the difference in their rotation rates:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ 2000 - 45 = 1955 \text{ degrees per minute} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m To find the time it takes for the two gears to complete one full rotation (360 degrees), we use the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ \text{Time} = \frac{360 \text{ degrees}}{1955 \text{ degrees per minute}} \approx 1.81 \text{ minutes} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Converting this time into seconds:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ 1.81 \times 60 \approx 108.6 \text{ seconds} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
