[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m [response] To determine how many positive integer values of \( n \) make \( 3^n \) a factor of \( 15! \), we need to count the number of times 3 appears as a factor in the prime factorization of \( 15! \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m First, we find the highest power of 3 that divides \( 15! \). This is done by summing the integer divisions of 15 by each of the powers of 3 from 1 to the largest power of 3 that is less than or equal to 15.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m The highest power of 3 in \( 15! \) is given by:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \left\lfloor \frac{15}{3} \right\rfloor + \left\lfloor \frac{15}{9} \right\rfloor + \left\lfloor \frac{15}{27} \right\rfloor = 5 + 1 + 0 = 6
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m So, \( 3^6 \) is the highest power of 3 that divides \( 15! \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Next, we need to find the number of times 3 appears as a factor in \( 15! \). This is given by the sum of the integer divisions of 15 by each of the powers of 3 from 1 to 6:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \left\lfloor \frac{15}{3} \right\rfloor + \left\lfloor \frac{15}{9} \right\rfloor + \left\lfloor \frac{15}{27} \right\rfloor = 5 + 1 + 0 = 6
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m So, \( 3^6 \) is the highest power of 3 that divides \( 15! \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
