[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ \text{Current height} = \text{Initial height} \times (1 + 0.05)^n \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Given that the current height is 452 cm and the increase is 5%, we can set up the equation:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ 452 = \text{Initial height} \times (1.05)^n \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m We need to find the initial height, which we will call \( h \). So we have:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ h \times (1.05)^n = 452 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m To find \( h \), we need to solve for \( h \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ h = \frac{452}{(1.05)^n} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m We know that \( n = 14 \) (since 2 weeks is 14 days), so we substitute \( n = 14 \) into the equation:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ h = \frac{452}{(1.05)^{14}} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Now we need to calculate \( (1.05)^{14} \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ (1.05)^{14} \approx 1.9215 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m So,
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ h = \frac{452}{1.9215} \approx 230.0 \]
