[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m [response] To determine how many cylindrical beakers are needed to fill a spherical tank, we first need to calculate the volume of the spherical tank and then find out how many cylindrical beakers are required to fill that volume.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m The volume \( V \) of a sphere is given by the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ V = \frac{4}{3} \pi r^3 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m where \( r \) is the radius of the sphere. For the given spherical tank, the radius \( r \) is 6 cm, so the volume of the sphere is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ V = \frac{4}{3} \pi (6)^3 = \frac{4}{3} \pi (216) = 288 \pi \text{ cubic cm} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Next, we need to find the volume of one cylindrical beaker. The volume \( V \) of a cylinder is given by the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ V = \pi r^2 h \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m where \( r \) is the radius of the cylinder and \( h \) is the height of the cylinder. For the given cylindrical beaker, the radius \( r \) is 3 cm and the height \( h \) is 8 cm, so the volume of one cylindrical beaker is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ V = \pi (3)^2 (8) = \pi (9) (8) = 72 \pi \text{ cubic cm} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m To find out how many cylindrical beakers are needed to fill the volume of the spherical tank, we divide the volume of the spherical tank by the volume of one cylindrical beaker:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ \text{Number of beakers} = \frac{288 \pi}{72 \pi} = \frac{288}{72} = 4 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Therefore, it will take \(\boxed{4}\) cylindrical beakers to fill the spherical tank.
