[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m assistant
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m [response] To find the speed of the particle, we need to determine the time derivative of the position vector \(\left( 3 \sin \frac{t}{4}, 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \right)\). The speed \(v(t)\) is given by the magnitude of the velocity vector, which is the derivative of the position vector with respect to time \(t\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m The position vector is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \mathbf{r}(t) = \left( 3 \sin \frac{t}{4}, 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \right)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m The velocity vector \(\mathbf{v}(t)\) is the derivative of \(\mathbf{r}(t)\) with respect to \(t\):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \mathbf{v}(t) = \left( \frac{d}{dt} \left( 3 \sin \frac{t}{4} \right), \frac{d}{dt} \left( 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \right) \right) = \left( 3 \cos \frac{t}{4} \cdot \frac{1}{4}, -3 \sin \frac{t}{4} \cdot \frac{1}{4} \right) = \left( \frac{3}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4}, -\frac{3}{4} \sin \frac{t}{4} \right)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m The speed \(v(t)\) is the magnitude of the velocity vector:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m v(t) = \sqrt{\left( \frac{3}{4} \cos \frac{t}{4} \right)^2 + \left( -\frac{3}{4} \sin \frac{t}{4} \right)^2} = \sqrt{\frac{9}{16} \cos^2 \frac{t}{4} + \frac{9}{16} \sin^2 \frac{t}{4}} = \sqrt{\frac{9}{16} (\cos^2 \frac{t}{4} + \sin^2 \frac{t}{4})} = \sqrt{\frac{9}{16}} = \frac{3}{4}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Thus, the speed of the particle is \(\boxed{\frac{3}{4}}\) unit of distance per unit of time
