[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m [response] To determine how many positive integer values of \( n \) make \( 3^n \) a factor of \( 15! \), we need to count the number of times 3 appears as a factor in the prime factorization of \( 15! \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m First, we find the highest power of 3 that divides \( 15! \). This is given by the sum of the integer divisions of 15 by each of the powers of 3 from 1 to the largest integer less than or equal to \( \frac{15}{3} \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \left\lfloor \frac{15}{3} \right\rfloor + \left\lfloor \frac{15}{3^2} \right\rfloor + \left\lfloor \frac{15}{3^3} \right\rfloor + \left\lfloor \frac{15}{3^4} \right\rfloor = 5 + 2 + 1 + 0 = 8
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m So, \( 3^8 \) is the highest power of 3 that divides \( 15! \). Therefore, \( 3^n \) is a factor of \( 15! \) if and only if \( n \leq 8 \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m The number of positive integers \( n \) such that \( n \leq 8 \) is \( 8 - 1 = 7 \).
