[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m [response] To determine how many cylindrical beakers are needed to fill a spherical tank, we first need to calculate the volume of the spherical tank and then divide that volume by the volume of one cylindrical beaker.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m The volume \( V \) of a sphere is given by the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ V_{\text{sphere}} = \frac{4}{3} \pi r^3 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m where \( r \) is the radius of the sphere. For the given radius \( r = 6 \) cm, the volume of the spherical tank is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ V_{\text{sphere}} = \frac{4}{3} \pi (6)^3 = \frac{4}{3} \pi (216) = 288 \pi \text{ cubic cm} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Next, we calculate the volume \( V \) of one cylindrical beaker. The volume \( V \) of a cylinder is given by the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ V_{\text{cylinder}} = \pi r^2 h \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m where \( r \) is the radius of the cylinder and \( h \) is the height of the cylinder. For the given radius \( r = 3 \) cm and height \( h = 8 \) cm, the volume of one cylindrical beaker is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ V_{\text{cylinder}} = \pi (3)^2 (8) = \pi (9) (8) = 72 \pi \text{ cubic cm} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m To find out how many cylindrical beakers are needed to fill the spherical tank, we divide the volume of the spherical tank by the volume of one cylindrical beaker:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m \[ \text{Number of beakers} = \frac{V_{\text{sphere}}}{V_{\text{cylinder}}} = \frac{288 \pi}{72 \pi} = \frac{288}{72} = 4 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=26424)[0m Therefore, the number of cylindrical beakers needed to fill the spherical tank is \(\boxed{4}\).
